  "knowledgePattern": "Turn a reciprocal integer equation into ordered factor pairs",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "number"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Because both reciprocals are positive, each is less than 1/9, so x &gt; 9 and y &gt; 9. Clearing denominators gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>81</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>The positive divisors of 81 are 1, 3, 9, 27 and 81. Taking each in turn as x − 9 fixes y − 9 uniquely, giving (x,y) = (10,90), (12,36), (18,18), (36,12), (90,10).</p><p>All satisfy the original equation and are positive integers. There are five ordered pairs. Answer A.</p>",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Clear the fractions",
      "body": "<p>Multiply the equation by 9xy and collect the product term and the two linear terms on one side.</p>",
      "recap": "Multiplying through by a common nonzero denominator clears fractions.",
      "pitfall": "x and y are positive, so neither denominator can vanish.",
      "pause": "Compare your expression with the expansion of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-algebraic-fraction-operations",
          "reminder": "Multiplying through by a common nonzero denominator clears fractions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn the problem into a divisor list",
      "body": "<p>First use the positive reciprocals in the original equation to decide whether x and y are above or below 9. Then list the factor pairs of the constant in your product equation.</p>",
      "recap": "Prime factors give a complete list of positive divisors.",
      "pitfall": "The pairs are ordered: swapping different x and y values gives another pair, but equal values do not.",
      "pause": "Recover x and y from each allowed pair of factors and count them.",
      "recall": [
